# The delivery form — the three shapes a form has, and the one thing that is not instant.

@checkout @functional
test "the delivery details go in as one block"
  open "/order"
  # Five fields, one step. The table is the statement: a row per field, named the way the page
  # names it, which is what a person reading the test has to be able to check against the page.
  fill form
    | "Email"    | "ada@example.com"  |
    | "Name"     | "Ada Whitfield"    |
    | "Address"  | "14 Bridge Street" |
    | "Town"     | "Bristol"          |
    | "Postcode" | "BS1 4ST"          |
  # The page echoes the town back, because `expect field "Town" equals "Bristol"` is `TF042`:
  # `equals` reads a value and a UI locator is not one. A form that shows what it took is both
  # what a delivery form does and the only thing a test can stand on.
  expect text "Delivering to Bristol" is visible

@checkout @functional
test "the choices a shop offers are a select and two checkboxes"
  open "/order"
  select "Filter" from field "Grind"
  tick field "Gift wrap this order"
  # `Send me offers` starts ticked, which is the only state in which unticking it means anything.
  untick field "Send me offers"
  expect text "Ground for Filter" is visible
  expect field "Gift wrap this order" is checked
  expect field "Send me offers" is not checked

@checkout @functional
test "saving is not instant, so the test waits for the answer and not for a clock"
  open "/order"
  click button "Save for later"
  wait until text "Your order is saved" is visible timeout wait 5s
